From: tokenscope — https://github.com/PS12007/tokenscope
Subject: [RFC] mul_mat: lower the chunking threshold from nth*4 to nth*2

Measured in FINDINGS F24. NOT APPLIED to the tree by bootstrap.py, and
deliberately kept out of 01-instrument.patch even though it touches a file in
bootstrap.py's TOUCHED list — this is a behaviour change to ggml's scheduler and
has nothing to do with the instrumentation. Apply it by hand to reproduce F24,
and revert it before regenerating 01:

    git -C ../llama.cpp apply ../tokenscope/patches/03-mulmat-chunk-threshold.patch
    ... measure ...
    git -C ../llama.cpp apply -R ../tokenscope/patches/03-mulmat-chunk-threshold.patch

What it does: `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat` chooses between work-stealing
chunks (threads pull from a shared atomic counter, so a slow core takes fewer)
and a static one-chunk-per-thread split. The threshold is `nth * 4`, and because
it contains `nth`, adding threads can push a matmul out of the self-balancing
mode. On a 24-layer F32 model at 16 threads that happens to `ffn_up` and
`ffn_gate` — the two largest nodes in the graph.

Measured on that model at 16 threads, i7-14700HX, Windows/MSVC:

  - arrival imbalance per unit work for `ffn_up` falls 0.210 -> 0.087, and the
    `ffn_up`/`ffn_down` ratio falls 0.967 -> 0.446, with non-overlapping ranges
    over 12 runs per arm
  - decode throughput +1.62% [+1.10, +2.15], t interval over six blocks in two
    independent runs, 240 rounds per arm, both arms uninstrumented and differing
    only in this line (FINDINGS F33; F24's +1.95% [+1.59, +2.35] came from a
    single run's bootstrap and was too narrow)
  - prefill throughput -1.30% [-2.67, +0.06] over the same six blocks, which
    contains zero but only just, and has been negative in every run. Prefill is
    above the threshold in both arms and cannot see this change, so a reviewer
    should treat the control as unclean rather than null. The arms also differ
    by ~1.1 MB of image, so code layout is not held constant and some unknown
    fraction of the decode gain may be layout rather than chunking

`ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id` carries the identical threshold at the same
file's line ~1698 and is deliberately NOT changed: it is the MoE path, no MoE
model was available to test it, and shipping an untested behaviour change is the
mistake FINDINGS F15 exists to record.

Caveats a reviewer should have: one machine, one thread count, two small dense
models, and no NUMA hardware — and the comment being edited says the `nth * 4`
form was tuned empirically on NUMA (PR #6915), which is exactly the case that
cannot be checked here. `2` is not argued to be the right constant; the finding
is that the constant is load-bearing and reachable by ordinary thread counts.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -1419,7 +1419,7 @@
     // If the chunking is poor for the number of threads on this setup, scrap the whole plan.  Re-chunk it by thread.
     //   Also, chunking by thread was measured to have perform better on NUMA systems.  See https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/6915
     //   In theory, chunking should be just as useful on NUMA and non NUMA systems, but testing disagreed with that.
-    if (nchunk0 * nchunk1 < nth * 4 || ggml_is_numa()) {
+    if (nchunk0 * nchunk1 < nth * 2 || ggml_is_numa()) {
         // distribute the thread work across the inner or outer loop based on which one is larger
         nchunk0 = nr0 > nr1 ? nth : 1; // parallelize by src0 rows
         nchunk1 = nr0 > nr1 ? 1 : nth; // parallelize by src1 rows
